  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and \nwetter conditions is expected this weekend into Monday. \n\nA series of cold fronts will bring about a stark change in the \nweather pattern compared to the above average heat we are seeing \ntoday. The first cold front will slowly pass through the area \novernight, then is expected to stall out over the Carolinas tomorrow \nand Sunday. This will be the weaker of the two fronts, likely thanks \nto a lack of upper level support. This initial push has a rather \nflat trough aloft with it, which will only be enough to take \nthe Mid- Atlantic from a ridge to nearly zonal flow. Isolated \nshowers associated with the system could impact the far northern\ncounties as early as this evening with increasing chances after\n2am. Scattered showers will continue to impact the northern \nhalf of the area through mid-day tomorrow before eventually \nexpanding southward later in the day. QPF for this first batch \nof showers is relatively low with most likely seeing less than \n0.5\". Since the FA should be north of the front and cloudy, \nrainy conditions are expected for most of the day, temps will be\nmuch cooler than today. Highs will only be in the low to mid \n60s along and north of I-64 and around 70 to the mid 70s to the \nsouth. \n\nA second cold front will approach the area Sunday into Monday. \nTropical moisture streaming up between the fronts will bring \nadditional rain to the area late Sat night through Sunday. The \ninitial batch of widespread precip likely moves in from the SW after \nmidnight Saturday into early Sunday morning, with categorical (80-\n90%) PoPs spreading E/NE through the day Sunday. Precip will \ngradually clear from NW to SE late Sunday through Monday morning as \nstrong high pressure builds in behind the second front. The trend of \nhigher rain totals for this weekend largely being confined to the \nthe southeastern half of the area has held with the 12z model\nsuite. NBM probs for >1\" of rain are 75-90% for areas along and\nSE of US- 360. Probs for more than 2\" are sitting at 50-60% for\nroughly the same area. Areas NW of US-360 could still see an \ninch or more, but probs are more like 30-50%. The QPF lines up \npretty well with this at 1-1.5\" SE of US-360 and 0.5-1\" \nnortheast of it. The chance for thunderstorms remains quite low \ndue to cool sfc temps, but a brief increase in CAPE across the \nfar SE Sunday afternoon could allow for an embedded storm or \ntwo. Speaking of temps: again cut a few degrees off the NBM \nguidance, with highs ranging from the mid- upper 60s NW to lower\n70s SE.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures and dry next week.  \n\nA strong area of cool high pressure (1020-1025mb) builds in behind \nthe second front while subtle troughing sets up aloft. High pressure \nremains in control for the bulk of the week, leading to a nice fall \nweek. Highs will stay in the mid 60s to mid 70s through the week, \nwhich is around the average for this time of year. Chilly overnight \ntemperatures are expected as well, especially for the piedmont where \ntemps in the 40s are expected all week. Statistical guidance even \nsuggests upper 30s in the piedmont for the Wed morning low.",
